D-Bus service activation: real `.service` file parsing, real spawning.

Closes TODO.md item 10. Before this, `servicedirs` was parsed by
`config/mod.rs` (with passing unit tests) and then never read anywhere else:
`list_activatable_names` returned `&[]`, `start_service_by_name` and
`update_activation_environment` both unconditionally returned
`Error::Failed("Service activation not yet supported")`, and no `.service`
file parser or spawn path existed in the crate at all.

MUST apply after `service-unknown-reply.patch` (alphabetical `*.patch`
glob order in `build.sh` already guarantees this: `s`-`e`-`r` < `s`-`t`-`a`).
The implicit-activation hook added here is a straight extension of that
patch's `send_msg`/`reply_service_unknown` code, and would fail to apply
against the unpatched tree.

What's added:

  * `src/config/service_file.rs` (new) -- parses the `[D-BUS Service]`
    section of a `.service` key file (`Name=`, `Exec=`, `User=`;
    `SystemdService=` and any other key is ignored, not an error). Never
    returns an `Err`: a missing section or missing `Name`/`Exec` is `None`,
    logged with `warn!` by the directory scanner and skipped, exactly like
    an unreadable file or directory. One bad `.service` file must never
    stop the bus from starting. Ships its own unit tests, including a
    real-filesystem `scan_dir` test.

  * `src/peers.rs` -- `Peers` gains `activatable_services` (name ->
    `ServiceFile`, scanned **once at startup**, not re-scanned lazily on a
    lookup miss -- see `load_servicedirs`'s doc comment for why that's
    enough for us: LeandrOS images are read-only per boot, `.service`
    files don't appear after boot, and it keeps every activation check a
    plain in-memory `HashMap` read) and `activation_environment` (from
    `UpdateActivationEnvironment`). `activate_service` spawns `Exec=` via
    `std::process::Command` and polls `name_registry` (bounded by
    `ACTIVATION_TIMEOUT` = 5s, `ACTIVATION_POLL_INTERVAL` = 50ms) for the
    spawned process to actually claim its name, returning the specific
    D-Bus error to report: `ServiceUnknown` (no `.service` file for the
    name), `SpawnExecFailed` (`Command::spawn` itself failed, or `Exec=`
    was empty), or `SpawnChildExited` (spawned fine but never claimed the
    name before the timeout).

  * `src/bus/mod.rs` / `src/bin/busd.rs` -- `Bus::load_servicedirs`
    delegates to `Peers::load_servicedirs`, called once from `main()` right
    after `Bus::for_address` succeeds and before `run()` starts accepting
    connections.

  * `src/fdo/dbus.rs` -- `list_activatable_names` now returns the union of
    the scanned service map and currently-owned well-known names (an
    already-running activatable service is still "activatable" per the
    spec). `start_service_by_name` returns `AlreadyRunning` if the name is
    already owned, otherwise calls `activate_service` and reports
    `Success`/its specific error -- never the old generic `Failed`.
    `update_activation_environment` stores the environment for future
    spawns instead of refusing.

  * Implicit activation (`src/peers.rs`, `send_msg`'s well-known-name
    branch): a plain method call to an unowned name should trigger
    activation per the spec, not just an explicit `StartServiceByName`.
    Implemented via `activate_if_activatable`, gated so the risk called
    out in TODO.md is contained: it first does a **synchronous, zero-cost
    HashMap lookup** (`is_activatable`) and returns `None` immediately, with
    no spawn attempt and no delay, for any name that isn't in the scanned
    service map. That is the overwhelming majority of unowned-name calls a
    real session makes (`service-unknown-reply.patch`'s own measurements:
    eleven distinct unowned names in the first ten seconds, mostly
    libcosmic single-instance probes to app IDs nobody ships a `.service`
    file for), so the fast `reply_service_unknown` path -- the fix for the
    hang that blocked every libcosmic app before that patch landed -- is
    completely unaffected for the non-activatable case: same lookup, same
    speed, same reply. Only a name that genuinely has a `.service` file
    pays the activation cost, and that cost (bounded by
    `ACTIVATION_TIMEOUT`) is inherent to implicit activation being useful
    at all, not a regression. On activation failure or timeout, the
    implicit path currently falls back to the same `ServiceUnknown` reply
    `reply_service_unknown` already sends for "nobody owns this" --
    slightly less precise than the specific `Spawn.*` error
    `StartServiceByName` returns for the same failure, but simplest and
    still correct: the caller gets an error back and does not hang, which
    is the property that matters here.

  * `User=` is parsed and carried on `ServiceFile`, but busd still has no
    setuid-helper/privilege-drop implementation (see
    `ports/dbus/session-pkg/session.conf`), so it can't actually be
    honoured beyond a `debug!` log; activation still spawns as the daemon's
    own user. LeandrOS's session bring-up is single-user/root-only today,
    so this doesn't change behavior in practice.

Verification performed (see AGENTS.md/README.md: this port is built and
tested outside the LeandrOS image, `cargo build`/`cargo test` against the
crate directly):

  * `cargo build` / `cargo test` (host, debug) against a **freshly
    re-extracted** `busd-0.5.0.crate` with all three `*.patch` files
    applied in the real `build.sh` order -- not just against the
    incrementally-edited working tree. All pre-existing tests still pass
    (config, fdo, greet, monitor, multiple_conns), plus this patch's own
    `src/config/service_file.rs` unit tests and a new
    `tests/activation.rs` integration suite (`Peers`-level: fast
    `ServiceUnknown` for an unknown name, `SpawnExecFailed` for a bad
    `Exec=`, `SpawnChildExited` timeout for a spawn that never claims its
    name, first-definition-wins on a duplicate `Name=` across two
    servicedirs; wire-level over a real `Bus` + `DBusProxy`:
    `ListActivatableNames`, `StartServiceByName` in all three outcomes
    (`ServiceUnknown`/`SpawnExecFailed`/`AlreadyRunning`),
    `UpdateActivationEnvironment`; and a regression guard that a plain
    method call to an unowned-but-activatable name spawns its `Exec=` and
    still gets an error reply rather than hanging).
  * `cargo +nightly build --release --target {aarch64,x86_64}-unknown-linux-musl`
    with the same `-C relocation-model=static` / `rust-lld` config
    `build.sh` uses -- both produce a statically-linked, stripped ET_EXEC
    `busd`, matching the existing shipped binaries' format (`file(1)`
    confirmed).

NOT verified: this has never been booted in QEMU. No LeandrOS image was
rebuilt, no real `.service` file was placed under the image's servicedir,
and no on-target activation (implicit or explicit) has been observed. The
`std::process::Command::spawn` path in particular is unverified against
LeandrOS's actual `fork`/`execve` behavior -- only against the host
(macOS) libc's.

